# msync() flushes whole pages of the mapped datafile, including the gaps
# between nodes inside the touched pages, which are never written/initialized.
# Such gaps are never read back, so the valgrind's complaint about the
# uninitialised msync() argument is a false positive.
{
   msync-uninit-page-holes
   Memcheck:Param
   msync(start)
   ...
   fun:osal_msync*
}

# The forked-children of the multithreaded tests (lck-contention, gh0201,
# gh0324, gh0329, and so on) intentionally terminate without any cleanup
# to exercise the post-fork/recovery scenarios, e.g. with the environment's
# locks still held. Valgrind performs the leak-check for such processes
# and then reports the leftovers as "possibly lost".

# TLS-blocks of the parent's threads that died at fork in the child,
# which is exactly the "possibly lost" signature of a fork-child.
{
   fork-child-thread-tls
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:calloc
   ...
   fun:allocate_stack*
   ...
   fun:pthread_create*
}

# The basal transaction's retired/repnl lists: pnl_alloc() returns an
# interior pointer (pnl+1), so these blocks are always reported as
# "possibly lost" when a process exits with an open environment.
{
   fork-child-basal-pnl
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:malloc
   fun:pnl_alloc*
   fun:txn_basal_create*
   ...
}

# A data page allocated (and filled) while creating a table, in a
# fork-child that exits without closing the environment.
{
   fork-child-page-from-tbl-create
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:malloc
   fun:page_shadow_alloc*
   ...
   fun:tbl_create*
   ...
}

# The dbi-name clones that the parent's churn-thread is freeing (outside the
# dbi_lock) at the moment of fork(): such a block is referenced only by the
# forking-away thread's stack/registers, which don't exist in the child,
# so the child reports it as definitely lost. The parent frees the block
# momentarily, and the main process leaves no such leftovers at all.
{
   fork-child-dbi-name-clone
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   fun:dbi_open_locked*
   ...
   fun:dbi_open_close*
   ...
}

# The shell-scripted tests (the tools' golden-runners and friends) are
# executed by bash, which is instrumented by valgrind as a traced child.
# Bash leaks some of its own parse/execute structures at exit - not our
# business, but such reports would fail the test.
{
   bash-self-leaks
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:*alloc
   obj:/usr/bin/bash*
   ...
}

# The shadow-pages parked in the env's shadow_reserve (an interior-pointers
# chain) at the exit of a faulted actor, which leaves its environment open.
{
   faulted-actor-shadow-pages
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:malloc
   fun:page_shadow_alloc*
   ...
}
